			)
		)
		(property "Maximum Working Voltage" "75 V"
			(at 0 0 0)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
				(hide yes)
			)
		)
		(property "Operating Temperature Range" "-55 °C to 175 °C"
			(at 0 0 0)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
				(hide yes)
			)
		)
		(property "Maximum Working Voltage Conditions" "Continuous DC or AC RMS. Lesser of √(P×R) or maximum working voltage. OCPM calculated nominal applied-voltage limit: 25.4695 V, from R=4990 Ω, P=0.13 W, and the 75 V manufacturer ceiling. Apply the stated power-rating conditions, derating and resistance tolerance; not an insulation rating."
			(at 0 0 0)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
				(hide yes)
			)
		)
		(property "Source Guidance" "For TNPW0402, the manufacturer separately publishes a terminal-part-temperature-controlled rating of 0.33 W at 105 °C; this does not replace the headline ambient P70 rating. Source quote: Rated dissipation, P 0.25 W 0.33 W 0.5 W 0.75 W 1 W 1.25 W at terminal part temperature, ϑt (3) 105 °C | Restriction applicable to the separate terminal-temperature rating. Source quote: (3) The specified maximum terminal part temperature must not be exceeded; compliance shall be ensured by appropriate thermal management and PCB design at the customer’s application level | General, Power, and Advanced are load/stability examples for the same resistor rather than different part variants; PCB thermal resistance must keep film temperature within the selected mode's limit. Source quote: The presented operation modes do not refer to different types of resistors, but actually show examples of different loads, that lead to different film temperatures and different achievable load-life stability (drift) of the resistance value. A suitable low thermal resistance of the circuit board assembly must be safeguarded in order to maintain the film temperature of the resistors within the specified limits. | For TNPW0402, the separate terminal-controlled alternative is 0.33 W at a terminal-part temperature of 105 °C. The terminal-temperature graph on page 7 shows linear derating to zero at 155 °C; this alternative is not the ambient P70 rating. Source quote: Rated dissipation, P 0.25 W 0.33 W 0.5 W 0.75 W 1W 1.25 W at terminal part temperature, ϑt (3) 105 °C | This restriction applies when using the separately published terminal-temperature rating. Source quote: The specified maximum terminal part temperature must not be exceeded; compliance shall be ensured by appropriate thermal management and PCB design at the customer’s application level | The 0.130 W ambient rating requires assembly thermal management sufficient to keep the film at or below 175 °C. Source quote: When the resistor dissipates power, a temperature rise above the ambient temperature occurs, dependent on the thermal resistance of the assembled resistor together with the printed circuit board. The rated dissipation applies only if the permitted film temperature is not exceeded. | General, power, and advanced modes are loading/stability examples for the same resistor; the headline 0.130 W P70 rating corresponds to advanced operation and must not be mixed with another mode’s temperature limits. Source quote: The presented operation modes do not refer to different types of resistors, but actually show examples of different loads, that lead to different film temperatures and different achievable load-life stability (drift) of the resistance value. A suitable low thermal resistance of the circuit board assembly must be safeguarded in order to maintain the film temperature of the resistors within the specified limits."
			(at 0 0 0)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
				(hide yes)
			)
		)
